  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The most reliable action when tests fail after a blue/green deployment is to <b>Programmatically detach the new Auto Scaling Group from the ELB and reattach the previous ASG to restore the application’s original state.</b> This option ensures an automated rollback, quickly minimizing downtime and restoring stable service with minimal manual intervention. </p><p>Why the other options are less ideal:</p><ul><li><i>Configure CloudWatch Alarms to notify the team upon failure and build a CloudWatch dashboard with ASG metrics.</i>—While these are excellent for monitoring and notification, they do not directly address rolling back the failed deployment, which is critical in a blue/green scenario.</li><li><i>Forward relevant logs to AWS CloudWatch Logs to aid in diagnosing the deployment failure.</i>—Logging is useful for troubleshooting but is more diagnostic than corrective. It doesn't actively restore service.</li><li><i>Set up SNS to alert your team so they can manually intervene in case of deployment errors.</i>—Relying on manual intervention can increase recovery time, leading to longer outages and potential customer impact. Automation is preferred for reliability and speed.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> For blue/green deployments, always design the rollback process to be automated and scriptable. This prevents human error and expedites system recovery.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your team has taken on maintenance of a new AWS application using a blue/green deployment model. All required AWS resources are managed via a CloudFormation stack. The application leverages an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) and maintains two Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs) for the current and incoming releases. Once the new Auto Scaling Group is linked to the ELB, automated tests are executed to verify the deployment. There’s a chance these tests could fail. You need to script an automated recovery process for such cases. Which of the following approaches should you implement when deployment validation fails?</p>",
